<p>Something remarkable is happening at the 2026 World Cup with aging athletes. The three most discussed goalkeepers — Vozinha (40), Neuer (40), and others — are all performing at or near their career level in their 40s. Messi at 38 leads the tournament in goals. Ronaldo at 41 is still scoring. Modrić at 40 played 200 international caps. The 2026 World Cup may be producing the most evidence on record that elite athletes at certain positions age more slowly than conventional sports science has historically suggested.</p>
<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>Can Cape Verde Do It Again?</h2>
<p>Cape Verde stopped Spain. Can they stop Argentina?</p>
<p>The tactical question is whether the same defensive structure that neutralized Spain works against a different kind of attacking team. Spain build possession patiently and create chances through repetitive combination play — the kind of attack where a well-organized low block with a reliable goalkeeper can hold on for 0-0. Argentina create chances differently: Messi in pockets, quick transitions, individual brilliance from Di María and Mac Allister alongside him. The angles of pressure are different.</p>
<p>The athletic reality is that Argentina have significantly more pace in transition than Spain. Where Spain took 27 shots against Vozinha because they were building methodically through a low block, Argentina are more likely to create 3-on-2 situations from turnovers that give Vozinha less time to set his positioning. The game-reading advantage that compensated for his aging reflexes against Spain is harder to apply when the ball is moving faster and earlier.</p>
<p>But Vozinha has already done what nobody thought possible once. The physical measurements on both sides of this matchup suggest Argentina should win comfortably. The physical measurements of the two previous Cape Verde matches suggested Spain and Saudi Arabia would score. <strong>Measurements predict outcomes until they don't.</strong></p>
